 ### Hardware-based Direct Volume Ray Casting
-A variant using programmable Digital Signal Processors (DSPs) to perform ray-casting, shading, and compositing on sample and gradient values generated by a geometry unit [S2]. Sample density and gradient values are transmitted from the geometry unit to the ray-casting units over a bus with a peak transfer rate of 240 MBytes/sec at 40 MHz [S2]. DSPs receive values from a FIFO buffer and compute light and material interaction for each volume element within 15 clock cycles [S2]. One or more scanlines are calculated per DSP and stored in local memory [S2]. A local master CPU collects all scanlines from DSP memories and transfers results to the host system, with no inter-DSP communication required for the current model [S2].
